A||||16||rodmcneill||Chamois or cotton towel?||||1||
Z||000000||rodmcneill||03-18-2001||10:48 AM||||I have noticed when drying my Fiero with a leather chamois that it tends to leave streaks. I had always heard that was supposed to be the best material for drying a car's finish. What do forum members use, are you a leather or a cotton person and we are still talking cars here!||||reg||1
Z||000001||BENGAL4||03-18-2001||11:00 AM||||I use the California Water Blade. It is a silicone squeegee, that cuts you drying time way down. It conforms to any shape on the car. I then use a chamois to get what the blade doesn't. I got it at Pep Boy's for $20.00. It's great.||||reg||10
Z||000002||Old Lar||03-18-2001||12:21 PM||||Cotton towles only...You can throw them into the washer when they get dirty.||||reg||10
Z||000003||stimpy||03-18-2001||12:25 PM||||I use one of those synthetic chamois. Blanking out right now on what they are called but tey come in a plastic tube to store it in. Anyhow, picks up a lot of water, is machine washable, and doesn't leave streaks. Very handy. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tina||03-18-2001||12:38 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>I use one of those synthetic chamois. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Same here.<P>Seems to pick up more water than the real thing.<BR>You need 2 though, you always seem to pick up<BR>a little dirt from somewhere halfway through.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tina.gif">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Tina (edited 03-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||KRMFiero||03-18-2001||12:54 PM||||the water blade thing is what i use, i bought it out of Jegs||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroLisa||03-18-2001||01:51 PM||||i use the water blade and then "the absorber" (that synthetic cloth that comes in the plastic tube)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tigger||03-18-2001||02:34 PM||||I use the water blade then a leather chamois.  rod, my chamois took about a dozen or so washes to break in and stop leaving streaks.||||reg||1
Z||000008||TheFiero64||03-18-2001||02:50 PM||||I get in and drive like hell to dry my car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><B>Just Kidding!</B>I've always used cotton towels.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||12
Z||000009||FieroJoe||03-18-2001||08:07 PM||||Ive always used cotton towels, but Ive always wanted to try one of those water blades.  I'll have to get one of those.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cheever2||03-18-2001||09:47 PM||||I was using real chamois but I use the Absorber plastic tube thing now, because it is MUCH better at picking up water.  Only problem is when it dries it becomes rock hard and doesn't become supple (sp?) again easily.  Maybe I'm supposed to put it back in the tube and not hang it to dry, I don't know.<P>Editing cause I'm sad that my man Eddie had to drop out of the race in Phoenix today.  Didn't see what took him out.  Get em next time, <A HREF="http://www.teamcheever.com/html/eddie.html" TARGET=_blank><B>Eddie!</B></A><P><BR>------------------<BR><I>Chuck Hodges</I><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 03-18-2001).]||||reg||6
Z||000011||FieroLisa||03-18-2001||11:10 PM||||Cheever- I noticed that too... one time i couldn't find the tube and it turned hard and gross. But I just soaked it again, wrung it out, and stuck it back in the tube- it seems to work better very slightly damp.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||03-19-2001||03:37 PM||||any chamois has to be slightly wet to work. I only use it to wipe up the biggest part then finish with terry towells myself. and at home I have the luxury of having a air compressor to blow dry in all the moldings, grills. If theres any dirt left, the chamois will rub it across finish, but towell will hold it in the weave. If you use towells too, best if you cut off the sewn/hemmed edges-they can scratch too.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex4mula||03-19-2001||04:02 PM||||"The Absorber" is the best thing I have ever used. Chamois just suck after you use the Absorber. But you cannot let it dry. Instructions say to put it back in the container wet (squeezed). I bet the blade thing should be good too but I'm worry that it will push dirt harder into the paint and thus be more prone to cause a scratch. I may be wrong...||||reg||1
Z||000014||BENGAL4||03-19-2001||04:55 PM||||I also use my air compressor to get water off of my wheels and all the cracks and crevices. It makes drying and preparing to wax, all the easier.<P>Bill||||reg||10
Z||000015||Cheever2||03-19-2001||06:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Instructions say to put it back in the container wet (squeezed).<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Oh.  I don't usually have my reading glasses on when I'm washing/polishing my car.  Thanks for passing along the info. <BR>***Now, where did I put that pastic tube?***<P><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000016||Tina||03-19-2001||08:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>***Now, where did I put that pastic tube?***</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lost that thing long time ago.<BR>I clip the DRY towel into the floormat holders at the carwash .Quick rinse does it, plus gets rid of dirt from last time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
